+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XBRWo1fmzoZn77dnjLLHNB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Phase 10: Recurring Tasks (Cron)\n2\t\n3\tAdd support for recurring scheduled tasks using simple cron-like expressions.\n4\t\n5\t## New Methods\n6\t\n7\t- **`schedule(queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate)`** - Create a recurring task schedule. Returns a schedule object with an `id`.\n8\t- **`unschedule(scheduleId)`** - Remove a schedule, stopping future occurrences.\n9\t- **`listSchedules(queueName?)`** - List all active schedules, optionally filtered by queue name.\n10\t- **`tick()`** - Manually advance and check schedules, triggering any that are due. For testing purposes.\n11\t\n12\t## Cron Expression Subset\n13\t\n14\tSupport a simple subset of cron expressions:\n15\t\n16\t| Expression       | Meaning              |\n17\t|------------------|----------------------|\n18\t| `*/N * * * *`    | Every N minutes      |\n19\t| `0 * * * *`      | Every hour (on the hour) |\n20\t| `0 0 * * *`      | Every day at midnight |\n21\t\n22\t## Requirements\n23\t\n24\t- `schedule()` returns a schedule object: `{ id, queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate, active: true }`\n25\t- `tick()` checks all active schedules and enqueues tasks for any that are due\n26\t- The `taskTemplate` is used as the payload for enqueued tasks\n27\t- `unschedule(scheduleId)` marks the schedule as inactive\n28\t- `listSchedules()` returns only active schedules\n29\t- Multiple schedules can coexist on the same or different queues\n30\t- Scheduled tasks are regular tasks once enqueued (subject to all other queue rules)\n31\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RHRBicVF9uaSx2wJ5fEg5h","session_id":"0f122924-a0ea-4945-8ed2-52b5e52c9c41","uuid":"7f4d93ea-eb36-4c8e-b9ba-470a53b5cfad","timestamp":"2026-06-30T20:58:51.020Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore project structure"}
+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EBPQ8e8v9RnEbonJNpLrtM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Phase 11: Middleware Pipeline\n2\t\n3\tAdd a middleware system for queue processing pipelines.\n4\t\n5\t## New Methods\n6\t\n7\t- **`use(queueName, middleware)`** - Add a middleware function to a queue's processing pipeline.\n8\t- **`process(queueName, handler)`** - Register a task handler for a queue and begin processing. The handler runs after all middleware.\n9\t\n10\t## Middleware Signature\n11\t\n12\t```typescript\n13\ttype Middleware = (task: Task, next: () => Promise<void>) => Promise<void>;\n14\t```\n15\t\n16\t## Requirements\n17\t\n18\t- Middleware functions run in the order they were added (first added = first to run)\n19\t- Each middleware must call `next()` to pass control to the next middleware (and eventually the handler)\n20\t- If a middleware does NOT call `next()`, the task processing is skipped\n21\t- Middleware can modify the task object (e.g., add metadata)\n22\t- If a middleware throws an error, the task is marked as failed\n23\t- Each queue has its own independent middleware stack\n24\t- Middleware receives the full task context (task object)\n25\t- `process()` dequeues a task and runs it through the middleware pipeline, then the handler\n26\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RHRBicVF9uaSx2wJ5fEg5h","session_id":"0f122924-a0ea-4945-8ed2-52b5e52c9c41","uuid":"b0fec268-bd76-4b6f-bdc8-3ff8bcbc676e","timestamp":"2026-06-30T20:58:51.021Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore project structure"}
